Input: {"question": "What was the courtesy name of Marquis Su?", "original_source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d00", "start": 0, "end": 177, "text": "Jia Kui (general) Jia Kui (174-228), originally named Jia Qu, courtesy name Liangdao, was a military general and official who lived during the late Eastern Han dynasty of China."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d00", "start": 178, "end": 248, "text": "He served under the state of Cao Wei during the Three Kingdoms period."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d00", "start": 249, "end": 360, "text": "Jia Kui was from Xiangling County (襄陵縣), Hedong Commandery (河東郡), which is present-day Xiangfen County, Shanxi."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d00", "start": 361, "end": 590, "text": "After appointments to several posts as prefect, administrator and Registrar to the Imperial Chancellor, Jia Kui was enfeoffed as a Secondary Marquis for his work in keeping his jurisdictions prepared for battle and well-supplied."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d00", "start": 591, "end": 635, "text": "During a skirmish with enemy forces from Cao"}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d01", "start": 0, "end": 281, "text": "[ lower-alpha 1 ] Spouse ( s ) Lady Liu Children Jia Chong Jia Hun Occupation General , official Courtesy name Liangdao ( 梁道 ) Posthumous name Marquis Su ( 肅侯 ) Peerage Marquis of Yangli Village ( 陽里亭侯 ) Original name Jia Qu ( 賈衢 ) This is a Chinese name ; the family name is Jia ."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d01", "start": 282, "end": 447, "text": "Jia Kui ( 174-228 ) , originally named Jia Qu , courtesy name Liangdao , was a military general and official who lived during the late Eastern Han dynasty of China ."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d01", "start": 448, "end": 491, "text": "He served under the state of Cao Wei during"}, "D3S1": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d02", "start": 0, "end": 641, "text": "dbt : People_of_Cao_Wei dcterms : subject dbc : Political_office-holders_in_Henan dbc : Politicians_from_Linfen dbc : Han_dynasty_generals_from_Shanxi dbc : Han_dynasty_politicians_from_Shanxi dbc : Political_office-holders_in_Hebei dbc :174_births dbc : Cao_Wei_politicians dbc : Generals_under_Cao_Cao dbc : Officials_under_Cao_Cao dbc :228_deaths dbc : Cao_Wei_generals rdf : type owl : Thing foaf : Person dbo : Person dul : NaturalPerson wikidata : Q19088 wikidata : Q215627 wikidata : Q5 wikidata : Q729 wikidata : Q82955 dbo : Animal dbo : Eukaryote dbo : Species schema : Person dbo : Politician rdfs : comment Jia Kui , ( 174 – ca ."}, "D3S2": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d02", "start": 642, "end": 665, "text": "230 ) , Liangdao ( 梁道 )"}, "D4S1": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d03", "start": 0, "end": 224, "text": "Jia Kui (Chinese: 賈逵 / 贾逵, Pinyin: Jiǎ Kuí, * 174 in today's Lifen, Shanxi; † around 230), courtesy name Fengyun, was a military general and bureaucrat who lived during the late Eastern Han Dynasty period of Chinese history."}, "D4S2": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d03", "start": 225, "end": 308, "text": "He later became a subject of the state of Cao Wei during the Three Kingdoms period."}, "D4S3": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d03", "start": 309, "end": 454, "text": "(de) Jia Kui (174–228), originally named Fengyun, was a general and civil servant of the late Chinese Han Dynasty and the subsequent Wei Dynasty."}, "D5S1": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d04", "start": 0, "end": 174, "text": "Wang Guan (Three Kingdoms) Wang Guan (died November or December 260), courtesy name Weitai, was an official of the state of Cao Wei during the Three Kingdoms period of China."}, "D5S2": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d04", "start": 175, "end": 208, "text": "Wang Guan lost his parents early."}, "D5S3": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d04", "start": 209, "end": 298, "text": "Cao Cao summoned him to serve as a Writing Assistant in the Imperial Chancellor's office."}, "D5S4": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d04", "start": 299, "end": 421, "text": "Later, he was reassigned to serve as the County Prefect (縣令) of Gaotang (高唐), Yangquan (陽泉), Zan (酂) and Ren (任) counties."}, "D5S5": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d04", "start": 422, "end": 562, "text": "When Cao Pi ascended the throne, Wang Guan was recalled to the capital to serve as a Gentleman of Writing (尚書郎) and Supervising Judge (廷尉監)."}, "D5S6": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d04", "start": 563, "end": 577, "text": "Afterwards, he"}, "D6S1": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d05", "start": 0, "end": 11, "text": "reckoning)."}, "D6S2": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d05", "start": 12, "end": 88, "text": "Sun Quan donned mourning garments and personally attended Gu Yong's funeral."}, "D6S3": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d05", "start": 89, "end": 189, "text": "He also honoured Gu Yong with the posthumous title \"Marquis Su\" (肅侯), which means \"serious marquis\"."}, "D6S4": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d05", "start": 190, "end": 282, "text": "Gu Yong was known for his abstinence from alcohol and for being a reserved and quiet person."}, "D6S5": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d05", "start": 283, "end": 382, "text": "He also maintained proper conduct and behaviour all the time, even in casual and informal settings."}, "D6S6": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d05", "start": 383, "end": 470, "text": "Sun Quan once said: \"Although Gu Yong doesn't talk much, he is spot on when he speaks.\""}, "D6S7": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d05", "start": 471, "end": 611, "text": "Whenever Gu Yong attended festive celebrations, he was usually a spoilsport because his mere presence often made his colleagues feel uneasy."}, "D6S8": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d05", "start": 612, "end": 616, "text": "They"}, "D7S1": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d06", "start": 0, "end": 45, "text": "do to become more like you , Master Guan Yu ?"}, "D7S2": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d06", "start": 46, "end": 104, "text": "'' `` You can not perfect the art of war by following me ."}, "D7S3": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d06", "start": 105, "end": 136, "text": "You must travel your own path ."}, "D7S4": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d06", "start": 137, "end": 186, "text": "'' `` I should search for my own inner strength ."}, "D7S5": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d06", "start": 187, "end": 210, "text": "Is that what you mean ?"}, "D7S6": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d06", "start": 211, "end": 245, "text": "'' `` I have nothing more to say ."}, "D7S7": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d06", "start": 246, "end": 301, "text": "It is time to let our skills in battle do the talking ."}, "D7S8": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d06", "start": 302, "end": 385, "text": "'' ~~Xu Huang and Guan Yu ; Dynasty Warriors 8 `` What a wonderful beard you have ."}, "D7S9": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d06", "start": 386, "end": 438, "text": "And you know what they say about men with beards ..."}, "D8S1": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d07", "start": 0, "end": 26, "text": "I am referring to Nandun ."}, "D8S2": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d07", "start": 27, "end": 138, "text": "'' [ 40 ] Sima Shi approved and sent Wang Ji to occupy Nandun ( 南頓 ; west of present-day Xiangcheng , Henan ) ."}, "D8S3": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d07", "start": 139, "end": 213, "text": "When Guanqiu Jian heard about it , he also led his forces towards Nandun ."}, "D8S4": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d07", "start": 214, "end": 398, "text": "After travelling about 10 li , he heard that Wang Ji had already beat him to it and occupied Nandun , so he retreated back to Xiang County ( 項縣 ; present-day Shenqiu County , Henan ) ."}, "D8S5": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d07", "start": 399, "end": 465, "text": "[ 41 ] Around the time , Deng Ai , the Inspector of Yan Province ,"}}, "original_cards": [{"doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d01", "card_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d01:c0", "entity": "Marquis Su", "scope": "courtesy name", "claim": "The courtesy name of Marquis Su was Liangdao.", "answer": "Liangdao", "quote": "[ lower-alpha 1 ] Spouse ( s ) Lady Liu Children Jia Chong Jia Hun Occupation General , official Courtesy name Liangdao ( 梁道 ) Posthumous name Marquis Su ( 肅侯 ) Peerage Marquis of Yangli Village ( 陽里亭侯 ) Original name Jia Qu ( 賈衢 ) This is a Chinese name ; the family name is Jia . Jia Kui ( 174-228 ) , originally named Jia Qu , courtesy name Liangdao , was a military general and official who lived during the late Eastern Han dynasty of China .", "original_document_number": 2}, {"doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d03", "card_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d03:c0", "entity": "Marquis Su", "scope": "courtesy name", "claim": "The courtesy name of Marquis Su was Fengyun.", "answer": "Fengyun", "quote": "Jia Kui (Chinese: 賈逵 / 贾逵, Pinyin: Jiǎ Kuí, * 174 in today's Lifen, Shanxi; † around 230), courtesy name Fengyun, was a military general and bureaucrat who lived during the late Eastern Han Dynasty period of Chinese history. He later became a subject of the state of Cao Wei during the Three Kingdoms period. (de) Jia Kui (174–228), originally named Fengyun, was a general and civil servant of the late Chinese Han Dynasty and the subsequent Wei Dynasty.", "original_document_number": 4}, {"doc_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d04", "card_id": "ramdocs-0303-b6e99ec183fa:d04:c0", "entity": "Marquis Su", "scope": "courtesy name", "claim": "The courtesy name of Marquis Su was Weitai.", "answer": "Weitai", "quote": "Wang Guan (Three Kingdoms) Wang Guan (died November or December 260), courtesy name Weitai, was an official of the state of Cao Wei during the Three Kingdoms period of China.", "original_document_number": 5}], "current_answers": ["Liangdao", "Fengyun", "Weitai"], "candidate_answers": [{"candidate_id": "R0", "answer": "Liangdao", "origin": "initial"}, {"candidate_id": "R1", "answer": "Fengyun", "origin": "coverage_addition"}, {"candidate_id": "R2", "answer": "Weitai", "origin": "coverage_addition"}], "requested_slot_hint": "other"}
